{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_75729873#12_135841954","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly answers how African rulers contributed: local rulers helped African merchants capture enslaved Africans, deliver them to Europeans for goods, and many continued participating for profit despite some opposition.","learned":"African rulers could help capture enslaved people, cooperate with merchants, exchange captives for gold/guns/goods, and participate because of profits; some opposed the trade."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414","reason":"The initial focused query produced a grade-3 result; continue down this result list for more documents with direct evidence about African rulers' role."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_13195298#6_19214269","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3414","rank":2,"snippet":": \" \" , \"segment\": \"The English began controlling the land that the Native Americans had used for hunting and farming. Atlantic slave trade Buying and selling of Africans for work in the Americas triangular trade European trade between the Americas, Africa, and Europe involving slaves and other goods middle passage Voyage that brought captured Africans to the West Indies and the Americas Why were slaves brought to the Americas? African people were brought to the Americas as slaves as enslaved labor force replacing the the Natives. They were replacing the Natives because they were dying to European diseases like small pox. The African people were stronger and healthier. How did some African rulers participate in the <cover>slave trade? African rulers </cover>had a major role in the slave trade. They benefited from the slave trade by capturing control of the slave trade. They Bring people inland and transport them to the coast to sell to European traders. What was the triangular trade?\" , \"start_char\": 3092.000000 , \"title\": \"\" , \"url\": \"https://quizlet.com/64036061/biggsa_fgch20-flash-cards/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"1/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_13195298#6_19214269","relevance":2,"reason":"The flashcard directly addresses how African rulers participated by bringing captives inland/coast and selling to European traders, but it is a user-generated study page with awkward/incomplete wording, so it is weaker evidence than an authoritative dedicated document.","learned":"Some study materials say African rulers captured or controlled captives inland, transported them to the coast, and sold them to Europeans."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414","reason":"The current query is still yielding direct answers about African rulers and the slave trade; continue to find more authoritative grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_75729873#11_135839513","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3414","rank":3,"snippet":"Passage DOCUMENT-BASED  QUESTIONS Slavery  in  the  Americas Consequences  of  the  Slave  Trade TERMS  &  NAMES USING  YOUR  NOTES MAIN  IDEAS CONNECT  TO  TODAY CRITICAL  THINKING  &  WRITING \" , \"segment\": \"Often,  slaves  were  captured prisoners  of  war,  or  people  of  a different  nationality  or  religion. However,  the  slavery  that developed  in  the  Americas  was based  largely  on  race. Europeans viewed  black  people  as  naturally inferior. Because  of  this,  slavery  in the  Americas  was  hereditary. The  Atlantic  World 567 African  Cooperation  and  Resistance Many  African  rulers  and  merchants played  a  willing  role  in  the  Atlantic  slave  trade. Most  European  traders,  rather than  travel  inland,  waited  in  ports  along  the  coasts  of  Africa. African  merchants, with  the  help  of  local  rulers,  captured  Africans  to  be  enslaved. They  then  deliv- ered  them  to  the  Europeans  in  exchange  for  gold,  guns,  and  other  goods. As  the <cover>slave  trade  grew,  some  African  rulers </cover>voiced  their  opposition  to  the  prac- tice. Nonetheless,  the  slave  trade  steadily  grew.\" , \"start_char\": 6736.000000 , \"title\": \"\" , \"url\": \"https://www.sps186.org/downloads/basic/734100/ch20_3.pdf\" }"}}
