{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_13195298#7_19215443","relevance":2,"reason":"This is another segment of the same Quizlet flashcard evidence already seen. It directly says African rulers had a major role by bringing people from inland to the coast to sell to European traders and connects this to triangular trade, but it is user-generated and poorly worded, so it is not strong grade-3 evidence.","learned":"Repeated Quizlet evidence: rulers helped move captives to the coast and sell them to Europeans within triangular trade."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414","reason":"Continue the focused result list because it has produced strong direct evidence, but watch for further duplicates and move to a new query if quality declines."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_102743421#6_243657082","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3414","rank":5,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_102743421#6_243657082\" , \"end_char\": 4725.000000 , \"headings\": \"Atlantic slave trade Atlantic slave trade Introduction Origins The Triangular Trade Opposition to and End of the Slave Trade \" , \"segment\": \"Thus, <cover>African rulers began selling males into the international slave trade. </cover>Most enslaved people were captured in raids on neighboring African peoples, though others became slaves because of criminal convictions or failure to pay debts. African rulers transported the captives to the coast and kept them in holding pens until they could be sold to European ship captains who sailed up and down the coast looking for slave cargo. Library of Congress, Washington, D.C. The second stage of the triangular trade was the shipment of enslaved people across the Atlantic Ocean, usually to Brazil or to an island in the Caribbean Sea. This trip, known as the Middle Passage, took a few weeks to several months. The ships were grossly overcrowded, with the captives wedged below decks and chained to platforms stacked in tiers. The average space allotted to an individual was "}}
