{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_102743421#6_243657082","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is an Atlantic slave trade/triangular trade document and directly explains African rulers' contribution: they sold captives into the international slave trade, acquired many through raids or other enslavement, transported captives to the coast, held them in pens, and sold them to European ship captains.","learned":"African rulers contributed by supplying enslaved people—often raided captives—transporting them to the coast, holding them, and selling them to European captains as part of triangular trade."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414","reason":"The current focused query continues to yield direct grade-3 evidence about African rulers' participation in the Atlantic/triangular slave trade, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3414"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_102743421#5_243655534","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3414","rank":6,"snippet":"\": \"Atlantic slave trade Atlantic slave trade Introduction Origins The Triangular Trade Opposition to and End of the Slave Trade \" , \"segment\": \"A smaller number came from Portuguese-controlled parts of southeastern Africa. The Atlantic slave trade changed the nature of African slavery. Within Africa, slave owners wanted primarily women and children for labor and for incorporation into their societies. They tended to kill males because they were troublesome and likely to flee. The Europeans, however, demanded primarily adult males to work in the New World colonies. Thus, <cover>African rulers began selling males into the international slave trade. </cover>Most enslaved people were captured in raids on neighboring African peoples, though others became slaves because of criminal convictions or failure to pay debts. African rulers transported the captives to the coast and kept them in holding pens until they could be sold to European ship captains who sailed up and down the coast looking for slave cargo. Library of Congress, Washington, D.C. The second stage of the triangular trade was the shipment of enslaved people across the Atlantic Ocean, usually to Brazil or to an island in the Caribbean Sea. This trip, known "}}
