{"latest_judgment":{"docno":null,"relevance":0,"reason":"The triangular-trade-specific result list is exhausted with no new unskipped document to judge.","learned":"The direct triangular trade query yielded a strong Britannica Kids result plus known duplicates, but no further results."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"african middlemen\" \"slave trade\" europeans captives)","reason":"Search for the common historical term 'African middlemen' to find documents explaining how African intermediaries/rulers supplied captives to Europeans in the Atlantic/triangular slave trade."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"african middlemen\" \"slave trade\" europeans captives)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1569081496#7_2643086018","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3423","rank":1,"snippet":"Capture and Captives\" , \"segment\": \"Europeans provided a host of tempting goods—textiles, ironware, exotic drinks, and firearms—all in exchange for African captives. Without <cover>African middlemen—local traders who had access to internal supplies of captured African peoples—Europeans could never have hoped to acquire more than small batches of Africans. Equally, without the commercial attractions of goods imported by the Europeans, African traders would have had little reason to secure ever more victims from their internal African suppliers. Underlying the commercial exchange of goods for people on the coast were unequal power relations between European and African traders and resulting warfare and violence among various African leaders who provided most of the captives sold to Europeans. Europeans formed alliances with such leaders, providing them with the weapons and means to attack rival African communities, in return for captives. African slavers such as the Asante and Dahomey emerged as powerful states and kingdoms in the eighteenth century, controlling and dominating interior slave trade </cover>routes in their respective territories. Widespread turbulence and upheaval resulted from the European demand for slaves, and the transatlantic slave trade stimulated an increase in slavery within Africa itself. Constant and "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"14/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1569081496#7_2643086018","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage is directly about African middlemen/leaders in the transatlantic slave trade and explains their contribution: local traders supplied captured Africans to Europeans, African leaders provided most captives sold to Europeans, alliances with Europeans supplied weapons for attacks on rival communities, and states such as Asante and Dahomey controlled interior slave-trade routes.","learned":"African rulers and intermediaries contributed by controlling internal supply routes, forming alliances with Europeans, using imported goods/weapons to raid rivals, and providing captives for sale on the coast."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3423","reason":"The African middlemen query produced strong direct evidence about African leaders' role in supplying captives and controlling trade routes, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
