{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ asante dahomey) \"slave trade\" captives europeans)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_536231563#2_1022888876","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3424","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_536231563#2_1022888876\" , \"end_char\": 3747.000000 , \"headings\": \"The Slave Trading Kingdoms Of Africa The Slave Trading Kingdoms Of Africa Origins and Development Of The Slave Trade In Africa Why Africans Were Involved In The <cover>Slave Trade The Slave Kingdoms Of Africa \" , \"segment\": \"In addition, Slavery was not completely foreign to Africa because the insitution already existed prior to the arrival of Europeans. For instance, Wars between African Kingdoms such as the Asante and Fante produced captives </cover>and prisoners of War that were treated as Slaves within African Society. Africans were also attracted to the consumer goods Europeans would exchange for Slaves, and whilst the Transatlantic Slave tried greatly increased the demand for Slaves, it was not responsible for introducing the concept of Slavery to Africa. Kingdoms such as the Ashanti and Dahomey Empires waged Wars to capture more Slaves and they grew enormous wealth and power from their involvement in theSlave Trade. Ultimately, it does not seem possible that the Transatlantic Slave Trade would have existed on such a large ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_536231563#1_1022886456","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
