{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_536231563#2_1022888876","relevance":3,"reason":"This passage directly addresses African slave-trading kingdoms and explains that wars among African kingdoms produced captives, European consumer goods attracted African participation, and kingdoms such as Ashanti/Asante and Dahomey waged wars to capture more enslaved people and gained wealth and power from involvement in the transatlantic slave trade.","learned":"African rulers and kingdoms such as Ashanti/Asante and Dahomey contributed by waging wars to capture enslaved people, exchanging them for European goods, and growing wealthy and powerful through the trade."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3424","reason":"The Asante/Dahomey query is producing direct evidence about specific African kingdoms' contributions to the slave trade; continue for more concrete grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3424"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_7315938#6_14404138","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3424","rank":3,"snippet":", \"segment\": \"Despite covering a large amount of land, Songhay eventually fell due to invasion. African Americans dominate in populations 1600 In the early 16th century, in cities like Lima, Peru, African Americans outnumbered the number of Europeans. At this point, slaves worked all possible occupations which <cover>Europeans didn't want to perform. Description: As more Africans are shipped, the areas in which they are sent become African dominant in population. Islamic concepts of slavery 1600 Around 1600, Sudanic states were introduced to Islamic concepts of slavery. Despite what Ahmad Baba said, Sudanic states enslaved their pagan and Muslim captives. Dutch becomes a major competitor in slave trade 1637 In result of growing plantations in the Americas, slave trade became more popular and other European countries competed. The Dutch seized El Mina from the Portuguese and partly Angola to supply in north eastern Brazil. Asante'</cover>s access to weapons 1650 from the Oyoko clan, the Asante obtained firearms.\" , \"start_char\": 3055.000000 , \"title\": \"African Slave Trade 1450-1750 Timeline | Preceden\" , \"url\": \"https://www.preceden.com/timelines/54992-african-"}}
