{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1931406995#5_2883411334","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage is from a dedicated Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade document and directly supports the answer: African leaders negotiated trade relations on their own terms, Kongo conflict produced captives traded to the Portuguese, and European powers formed military alliances with African groups that encouraged warfare to produce captives for the slave trade.","learned":"African rulers contributed by negotiating trade with Europeans, becoming involved in conflicts and alliances that produced captives, and supplying those captives to Portuguese and other European traders."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3430","reason":"The African leaders query is still producing direct evidence from dedicated trans-Atlantic slave trade material; continue to seek more grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3430"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_2159106972#5_2432347332","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3430","rank":5,"snippet":"trade Creole women in the French Caribbean General overview Anglo-American institution of slavery Abolition See also References Bibliography External links \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Slave trade In this mercantilist economy of the French trans-Atlantic trafficking of enslaved human beings from Africa, wealth and goods were moved in an insular, unidirectional fashion to the exclusive benefit of Europe. In fact, the French had a policy coined “the Exclusif” ( exclusive in English), requiring French colonies to only sell exports to France and purchase imported goods from France. This promoted the concept of “centripetal trade” in which all profit and capital spread amongst the American colonies eventually circulated back into the hands of European powers. The trafficking of enslaved people was just one faction of the mercantillist economy. In addition, Europeans brought “pacotille” or “cheaply made European goods” to trade with Africans. This often took the form of colonial products such as sugar, rum, tobacco, coffee, or indigo. Thus African leaders, who themselves were in control of selling African captives </cover>with Europeans, did not retain the wealth they acquired in the trafficking of enslaved people. Rather they were the targeted customers of poorly-made pacotille. Their profits from "}}
