{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"local chiefs\" \"slave trade\" europeans captives)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_1145489821#9_1895594768","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3433","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_1145489821#9_1895594768\" , \"end_char\": 6864.000000 , \"headings\": \"How were Africans captured for the slave trade? How were Africans captured for the <cover>slave trade?\" , \"segment\": \"On the west and southwest coast of Africa the local chiefs did raid their neighbour tribes villages and provided the Europeans with their captives. </cover>When they ran out of enemies to raid, they often sold their own people. Greed does not know limits 4K views · View upvotes Justin Whitmar , studied History & Geography at Buffalo Grove High School (2017) Answered 3 years ago · Author has 132 answers and 127.3K answer views Most of the time it wasn’t the slave traders who were catching the slaves, it was african kings who wanted money. They captured their countrymen and then gave them to the Portugese or British in exchange for guns. Then the Africans used guns against each other to capture other kingdom’s citizens for slaves. By the time this manipulation monstrosity was over, the slave traders could just reproduce the slaves they had even if "}}
