{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"african rulers\" (+ guns goods) slaves europeans)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#3_505308024","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3437","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#3_505308024\" , \"end_char\": 3093.000000 , \"headings\": \"Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade<cover>Europeans Slaves for Guns Kidnapped and Incarcerated References and Further Reading \" , \"segment\": \"Forts built  by the Portuguese and Dutch on the Gold Coast (modern Ghana)  were captured by the British in 1667. Slaves for Guns West African rulers </cover>were instrumental in the slave trade. They exchanged their prisoners of war (rarely their own people)  for firearms manufactured in Birmingham and elsewhere in Britain. With their newly acquired weapons, kings and chiefs were able  to expand their territories. The slave trade had a profound  effect on the economy and politics of West Africa, leading,  in many cases, to an increase in tension and violence. Europeans Repay African Hospitality Document Black People and 'Mulattoes' Employed  by the Royal African Company (162KB) Document | Transcript In 1650, for example, Dahomey, a small coastal state  on the Atlantic, extended its borders into the interior of  Africa. Half a century later, the Asante Empire under Osei  Tutu "}}
