{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_5134399#3_8165976","relevance":2,"reason":"The flashcard directly answers that African rulers delivered enslaved people to Europeans in exchange for gold, guns, and other goods, motivated by profits, but it is user-generated study material and not a strong dedicated evidentiary source.","learned":"Study materials repeat the core answer: African rulers participated for profit by delivering enslaved people to Europeans in exchange for gold, guns, and goods."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437","reason":"The broader African rulers/guns/goods query has already produced one strong grade-3 source and may contain more documents directly explaining exchange of captives for European goods, so continue the list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#1_505304969","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3437","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#1_505304969\" , \"end_char\": 1742.000000 , \"headings\": \"Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade<cover>Europeans Slaves for Guns Kidnapped and Incarcerated References and Further Reading \" , \"segment\": \"An Interview with the King of Sestro Document European Forts in Africa (155KB) Document | Transcript During  the 16th century the first foundations of globalisation were  laid when African rulers </cover>forged relationships with European  traders. One early English explorer was William Hawkins, father  of John Hawkins . In the 1530s, Hawkins made voyages to Guinea to obtain ivory, dyewoods and gold. At this stage the English seemed to have little interest  in taking slaves. This, however, was soon to change. There was intense rivalry for West Africa among Europeans. With no interest in conquering the interior, they concentrated  their efforts to obtain human cargo along the West African  coast. During the 1590s, the Dutch challenged the Portuguese  monopoly to become the main slave trading nation. Later, Scottish,  Swedish and Danish African companies registered their interest. With so many European powers on "}}
