{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#1_505304969","relevance":2,"reason":"This segment is from a dedicated 'Africa and the Atlantic Slave Trade' document and notes that African rulers forged relationships with European traders while Europeans sought human cargo on the West African coast. However, the snippet itself gives only a general setup and does not directly state the specific mechanisms of rulers capturing, selling, or exchanging captives.","learned":"Some sources frame African rulers' contribution as forming trading relationships with European merchants on the West African coast, which later enabled the slave trade."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437","reason":"The current query has produced a strong dedicated source about West African rulers exchanging captives for guns, and additional segments may contain more direct evidence from the same or other documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_33_1468078576#2_3121905745","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3437","rank":4,"snippet":"\"Cowrie shells Cowries, oval shells of different sizes with serrated edges, were one of the largest commodities imported by the British. They became a vital part of the system of exchange between Europeans and African traders. Slavers paid around 25,000 shells for an enslaved African. The impact of the gun trade The mass introduction of firearms was the single most significant technological innovation brought by Europeans to West Africa. Kings and warlords were anxious to trade with <cover>Europeans to acquire guns. Twenty million guns were imported to Africa in the second half of the 18th century. Many guns imported by the British were manufactured in Birmingham. African rulers exchanged captives for guns. Rulers were in competition with each other, so the acquisition of guns provided an edge over their rivals. This increased their drive to capture and sell slaves.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 1565.000000 , \"title\": \"Effects of the trade on African societies in West Africa - Implications of the slave trade for African societies - Higher History Revision - BBC Bitesize\" , \"url\": \"https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zxt3gk7/revision/2\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"32/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_33_1468078576#2_3121905745","relevance":3,"reason":"The BBC Bitesize revision page directly explains how African rulers contributed: kings and warlords traded with Europeans to acquire guns, African rulers exchanged captives for guns, and competition for firearms increased their drive to capture and sell enslaved people.","learned":"African rulers helped feed the slave trade by exchanging captives for European firearms; access to guns intensified rivalries, warfare, and further capture and sale of enslaved people."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437","reason":"The African rulers/guns/goods query is producing strong direct evidence from educational sources about rulers exchanging captives for European goods; continue to find more grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#0_505303192","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3437","rank":6,"snippet":" , \"headings\": \"Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade Europeans<cover>Slaves for Guns Kidnapped and Incarcerated References and Further Reading \" , \"segment\": \"The National Archives | Exhibitions & Learning online | Black presence | Africa and the Caribbean Africa and  the Atlantic Slave Trade Europeans Before the 16th century, Europeans were not deeply involved  in slave trading on the West African coast. However, there  was some movement of African labour to Madeira and the Canary  Islands by the early Portuguese explorers from 1470 onwards. The Portuguese were also the first to use African slave labour  in gold mines, and on sugar plantations on the small equatorial  island of São Tomé. These plantations became  the model for future sugar estates in the West Indies. African  exports at this time included gold, palm oil, nuts, yams,  pepper, ivory, gum and cloth. An Interview with the King of Sestro Document European Forts in Africa (155KB) Document | Transcript During  the 16th century the first foundations of globalisation were  laid when African rulers </cover>forged relationships with European  traders. One early English explorer was William Hawkins, father  of John Hawkins . In the 1530s, Hawkins made ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_13195298#7_19215443","rank":5,"relevance":2}]}}
