{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_263612873#0_505303192","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a near-duplicate segment from the National Archives 'Africa and the Atlantic Slave Trade' page already seen. The document is dedicated to the topic and contains direct evidence that African rulers forged relationships with European traders and, in its 'Slaves for Guns' section, exchanged prisoners/captives for firearms.","learned":"Repeated National Archives evidence: African rulers built trading relationships with Europeans and contributed by exchanging captives or prisoners of war for guns, which strengthened rulers and intensified conflict."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437","reason":"The African rulers/guns/goods query is still producing direct educational evidence about rulers exchanging captives for European goods; continue to look for more non-duplicate grade-3 sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3437"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1587375101#2_3338344921","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3437","rank":7,"snippet":"out to be mythical silver mines in the west. Dias established himself as captain-general, or governor, in Luanda, with jurisdiction over an undefined area between the Dande and Kwanza rivers. A few years after his arrival a century of almost constant warfare was initiated. The wars soon resolved themselves into slave-raiding campaigns, as <cover>Europeans demanded labour rather than tropical products in exchange for their merchandise, and African societies rapidly exhausted local supplies of war captives and criminals. Chiefs exchanged slaves for European firearms and luxury goods and secured further dependents with cheaply produced textiles and Brazilian alcohol. Impelled by the increased demand for slaves for the sugar plantations of São Tomé and later Brazil, and relying on African mercenaries and allies, the military governors of Luanda launched armed incursions against the people of the interior. States rose and fell as African rulers </cover>were ineluctably drawn into the slave trade and were as often destroyed by it.\" , \"start_char\": 1707.000000 , \"title\": \"Southern Africa - European and African interaction from the 15th through the 18th century | Britannica\" , \"url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/"}}
