{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_959921538#0_1870265467","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly says the rulers of Kongo were among early African Christian converts and that Kongo was one of the most prolific suppliers of captives to the Portuguese slave trade, but it is a general Kingdom of Kongo/Bible timeline article rather than a dedicated, authoritative explanation of African rulers' contribution to the triangular trade.","learned":"Kongo rulers and the kingdom are described as major suppliers of captives to the Portuguese trans-Atlantic slave trade."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ kongo \"trans-atlantic slave trade\" captives portuguese \"african leaders\")","reason":"Search for a more authoritative/dedicated source on Kongo and Portuguese trade that directly explains how African leaders/rulers supplied captives through alliances, conflict, and trade."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ kongo \"trans-atlantic slave trade\" captives portuguese \"african leaders\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1931406995#7_2883415289","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3445","rank":3,"snippet":"7834.000000 , \"headings\": \"The Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade The Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade ESTABLISHING THE TRADE \" , \"segment\": \"For example, the Portuguese colonized portions of Angola in 1571 with the help of military alliances from <cover>Kongo, but were pushed out in 1591 by their former allies. Throughout this early period, African leaders and European competitors ultimately prevented these attempts at African colonization from becoming as extensive as in the Americas. The Portuguese dominated the early trans-Atlantic slave trade on the African coast in the sixteenth century. As a result, other European nations first gained access to enslaved Africans through privateering during wars with the Portuguese,rather than through direct trade. When English, Dutch, or French privateers captured Portuguese ships during Atlantic maritime conflicts, they often found enslaved Africans on these ships, as well as Atlantic trade goods, and they sent these captives </cover>to work in their own colonies. In this way, privateering generated a market interest in the trans-Atlantic slave trade across European colonies in the Americas. After Portugal temporarily united with Spain in 1580, the Spanish broke up the Portuguese slave trade monopoly by offering direct ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1931406995#6_2883413104","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1931406995#5_2883411334","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
