{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1931406995#7_2883415289","relevance":2,"reason":"This segment is from a dedicated Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade document and is related to African leaders' role through Kongo-Portuguese military alliances and African resistance to colonization, but the snippet does not directly explain rulers supplying, capturing, transporting, or selling captives, unlike other segments from the same source.","learned":"Kongo and other African leaders could enter military alliances with Portuguese traders/colonizers and also limit European colonial control, but this specific segment is less direct about supplying enslaved captives."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ dahomey kings \"slave trade\" europeans captives)","reason":"Search for a specific major slave-trading kingdom and rulers using direct terms—Dahomey kings, Europeans, and captives—to find concrete evidence about African rulers capturing/selling people into the Atlantic/triangular trade."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ dahomey kings \"slave trade\" europeans captives)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_202970661#8_227873821","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3446","rank":1,"snippet":"the rivalry between the kingdom and the city of Porto-Novo, and the continued tribal politics of the northern region, persisted into the colonial and post-colonial periods. The Dahomey Kingdom was known for its culture and traditions. Young boys were often apprenticed to older soldiers, and taught the kingdom's military customs until they were old enough to join the army. Dahomey was also famous for instituting an elite female soldier corps, called Ahosi, i.e. the king's wives, or Mino, \"our mothers\" in the Fon language Fongbe, and known by many <cover>Europeans as the Dahomean Amazons. This emphasis on military preparation and achievement earned Dahomey the nickname of \"black Sparta \" from European observers and 19th-century explorers such as Sir Richard Burton. Early Portuguese colonization and the slave trade The Portuguese Empire was the longest colonial presence in Benin, beginning in 1680 and ending in 1961, when the last forces left Ajudá. The kings of Dahomey sold their war captives </cover>into transatlantic slavery.\" , \"start_char\": 6127.000000 , \"title\": \"Benin - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benin\" }"}}
